llama.cpp (as fetched by Ollama v0.35.1, llama.cpp b11232, applied after
126): on gfx803, a prompt matmul whose output rows are a multiple of 32 KiB
apart writes into padded rows first.
gfx803-specific: engages only where patch 106 dequantizes into padded rows
(GCN4).

WHY
---
Patch 106 runs quantized prompt matmuls as fp32 GEMMs on the base image's
gfx803 SGEMM and pads the weight and activation rows by 16 floats, because
row strides that are multiples of 8 KiB put every row of a GEMM tile in one
DRAM channel. The output keeps its natural stride, the matmul's ne0. With
ne0 = 8192 (a Qwen3.6 35B-A3B DeltaNet input projection, 40 calls per
2048-token batch) the columns of C lie 32 KiB apart, and the SGEMM's stores
then all land in one channel: on an RX 590, 8192 x 2048 x 2048 TN takes
17.6 ms with the 64x64 tile and 19.0 ms with the two-wavefront tile, against
14.7 ms with the two-wavefront tile when the columns are 16 floats further
apart. The same GEMM with its stores removed takes 14.0 ms at 8064, 8192
and 8320 rows alike, so the loss is in the stores alone.

WHAT
----
In ggml_cuda_mul_mat_cublas_impl, when patch 106's padded dequantization is
in use, the call is a single fp32 GEMM with more than one column, and ne0
floats are a multiple of 32 KiB, the GEMM writes into a pool buffer with
rows of ne0 + 16 floats and cudaMemcpy2DAsync moves the result to dst
(0.65 ms for 8192 x 2048). The values are the same; only where the GEMM
stores them changes. Strides of 8 and 16 KiB (2048 and 4096 rows) gained
nothing measurable from the padding and keep the direct store.

Measured on an RX 590 (Qwen3.6 35B-A3B Q4_K_M, every expert in system RAM,
llama-bench -ncmoe 99, 2048-token batches, HSA_ENABLE_SDMA=1
ROC_CPU_WAIT_FOR_SIGNAL=1, with the base image's two-wavefront fp32 tile,
two alternating rounds): pp2048 602.5 / 602.0 -> 615.8 / 615.3 tok/s
(+2.2%). llama-perplexity at 2048-token batches gives per-chunk values
equal to those of the build without the patch. Through Ollama (2,800-token
prompt, one 2048-token batch and one of 752, together with the base
image's fp32 two-wavefront tile, two rounds): prompts 516.8 / 515.8 ->
518.8 / 518.5 tok/s, generation unchanged, greedy output identical on
Qwen3.6 35B-A3B and Qwen3 8B. The 752-token batch gains nothing: the
two-wavefront tile takes only column counts that are multiples of 128,
and a batch that short spends most of its time on the expert copies.

